> In any case, all that is needed to do a correct calculation is the true
> effective probability of generation of the potions we are talking about,
> rolling all the effects you mention into one number, and the true
> average number of potions found per level. If this is evaluated from
> about 1000 games, I think one can make pretty reliable statements.  What
> your discussion suggests, then, is that these true probabilities are far
> higher for *healing potions than given nominally (unless the high
> abundance of object detection does not reduce them back to their
> original value :-) ).

I think your estimate would be much stronger if your function summed
two different functions:

floor_generation: expected total number of potions generated on the
floor of this level * PROB(potion_type).

(I would include some estimate of probabilities of shops which can
have potions and how many they'll have.  Definitely include
probability of generating a container containing one/more potions as
well.  These numbers are all readily available without source diving.)

monster_generation: Some reasonable estimate of the number of monsters
which will be generated and their levels will be needed.  Calculate an
expected number of potions generated on those which can have defensive
items.  Add a number of potions (based on potion frequency and
PROB(potion_type) expected from death drops.

Sum both across all levels visited, and you'll have a much better
approximation of what you'd expect to see.

The biggest problem, as I see it, with your original method is that it
lumps together two things with wildly different distributions.  Rather
than try to unify them (with notable loss of precision), I think it
would be simpler (and certainly more accurate) to treat them as
separate numbers.


